+# Timeout propagation research
+
+## Web research
+
+The web search for "HTTPX timeouts" was fetched via a Bing SERP because the
+`web_search` / Brave tool was unavailable in the environment. Results included:
+
+- https://www.python-httpx.org/
+- https://pypi.org/project/httpx/
+- https://github.com/encode/httpx
+- https://betterstack.com/community/guides/scaling-python/httpx-explained
+
+The top GitHub result, `projectdiscovery/httpx`, is a different project (an HTTP
+probing tool), not python-httpx (the `encode/httpx` Python HTTP client library
+this checkout belongs to).
+
+## Pinned upstream revision
+
+This checkout tracks encode/httpx at pinned commit
+`26d48e0634e6ee9cdc0533996db289ce4b430177`. Upstream sources referenced:
+
+- https://github.com/encode/httpx/blob/26d48e0634e6ee9cdc0533996db289ce4b430177/httpx/_client.py
+- https://github.com/encode/httpx/blob/26d48e0634e6ee9cdc0533996db289ce4b430177/httpx/_config.py
+- https://github.com/encode/httpx/blob/26d48e0634e6ee9cdc0533996db289ce4b430177/httpx/_types.py
+
+## Compatibility contract
+
+- A Timeout value has exactly four independent dimensions: connect, read, write, pool. Each is float | None; None disables that dimension (no timeout), a float is a timeout in seconds, and the four never cross-contaminate.
+- Normalization: Timeout(scalar float) => all four = scalar; Timeout(None) => all four = None; Timeout(scalar, dim=v, ...) => each dimension uses its explicit v when given, else the scalar; Timeout(instance) copies all four fields; Timeout((c, r, w, p)) positional order = connect/read/write/pool (missing trailing => None); Timeout() with no scalar and not all four explicit => ValueError.
+- Precedence: the request-level timeout parameter defaults to the UseClientDefault sentinel (not None). build_request resolves timeout = self.timeout if UseClientDefault else Timeout(timeout), then extensions["timeout"] = timeout.as_dict(). Therefore an explicit per-request timeout (including explicit None) wins over the client timeout; an omitted request timeout inherits the client timeout. send() backfills the client default via _set_timeout only when request.extensions has no "timeout" key.
+- Default: DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_CONFIG = Timeout(5.0) => connect=read=write=pool=5.0.
+
+## Local divergences and fixes
+
+Two local divergences from the pinned upstream behavior were found.
+
+### Fix 1 — tuple normalization in `httpx/_config.py`
+
+`Timeout.__init__` mapped a four-field tuple in the order
+`(connect, read, write, pool)`, but the tuple branch assigned the third field to
+`write` using `timeout[1]` instead of `timeout[2]`, so `write` copied `read` and
+was never independently settable.
+
+Change made:
+
+```python
+# before
+self.write = None if len(timeout) < 3 else timeout[1]
+# after
+self.write = None if len(timeout) < 3 else timeout[2]
+```
+
+`pool` already used `timeout[3]`, so it was left unchanged.
+
+### Fix 2 — `_set_timeout` in `httpx/_client.py`
+
+`BaseClient._set_timeout` is the send-path fallback used when a raw
+`httpx.Request` has no `"timeout"` extension. It computed but discarded the
+client default and stamped the request with `Timeout(None).as_dict()`, disabling
+all timeouts. It also contained a dead `isinstance(self.timeout,
+UseClientDefault)` computation because `self.timeout` is always a `Timeout`
+instance by the time send is reached.
+
+Change made:
+
+```python
+def _set_timeout(self, request: Request) -> None:
+    if "timeout" not in request.extensions:
+        request.extensions = dict(**request.extensions, timeout=self.timeout.as_dict())
+```
+
+This preserves an existing `request.extensions["timeout"]` (so an explicit
+request timeout, including explicit `None`, wins) and otherwise inherits the
+client default `self.timeout`.
+
+`BaseClient.build_request` already implemented the correct precedence — explicit
+request timeout wins, omitted request timeout inherits the client default — and
+was left unchanged.
